1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,493
Every command works with three streams: input, normal output, and error output.

2
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:08,081
Redirection decides where each one goes.

3
00:00:08,551 --> 00:00:09,556
So in this video:

4
00:00:09,576 --> 00:00:14,135
use input-output redirection, with greater-than, double greater-than, pipes,

5
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:15,401
and two greater-than.

6
00:00:19,421 --> 00:00:22,004
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:22,474 --> 00:00:24,234
Use input-output redirection:

8
00:00:24,254 --> 00:00:28,470
greater-than, double greater-than, pipe, two greater-than, and so on.

9
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:32,838
We'll send output to files, append to them, chain commands together,

10
00:00:32,858 --> 00:00:35,085
and separate errors from normal output.

11
00:00:42,605 --> 00:00:48,055
Three streams matter. Standard input is stream zero. Standard output is stream one.

12
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:49,966
Standard error is stream two.

13
00:01:01,436 --> 00:01:04,056
By default, input comes from the keyboard,

14
00:01:04,076 --> 00:01:08,456
and both output and errors go to the screen. Redirection changes that.

15
00:01:17,976 --> 00:01:22,622
Greater-than sends standard output to a file, and replaces whatever was there.

16
00:01:22,642 --> 00:01:26,511
We write first line, then second line, and the first one is gone.

17
00:01:35,981 --> 00:01:38,123
Double greater-than appends instead.

18
00:01:38,143 --> 00:01:41,187
Now the file holds the second line, then the third.

19
00:01:49,707 --> 00:01:53,989
A pipe connects one command's output to the next command's input.

20
00:01:54,009 --> 00:01:58,092
Here L S lists slash etc, and head keeps the first five lines.

21
00:02:07,062 --> 00:02:09,093
And W C dash L counts lines.

22
00:02:09,113 --> 00:02:13,634
Slash etc has one hundred eighty-three entries on this system.

23
00:02:20,154 --> 00:02:24,120
Now errors. L S is asked for a missing path and a real one.

24
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:27,430
Both messages land on the screen, mixed together.

25
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,937
Two greater-than redirects standard error.

26
00:02:38,957 --> 00:02:43,669
Normal output goes to good dot t x t, errors go to bad dot t x t,

27
00:02:43,689 --> 00:02:45,416
and we print both files.

28
00:02:58,936 --> 00:03:02,646
To send both streams to one file, redirect output first,

29
00:03:02,666 --> 00:03:06,310
then add two greater-than ampersand one. Order matters:

30
00:03:06,330 --> 00:03:08,708
output first, then errors follow it.

31
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:22,623
To throw output away, send it to slash dev slash null.

32
00:03:22,643 --> 00:03:27,819
The error is gone from the screen, but the exit status still says it failed: two.

33
00:03:38,339 --> 00:03:41,658
Less-than feeds a file to a command's input.

34
00:03:41,678 --> 00:03:45,679
W C dash L reads out dot t x t, and counts two lines.

35
00:03:53,149 --> 00:03:56,595
tee writes to a file and also shows the output on screen.

36
00:03:56,615 --> 00:03:58,420
Handy in the middle of a pipe.

37
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:10,376
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

38
00:04:10,846 --> 00:04:13,606
Files live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

39
00:04:13,626 --> 00:04:16,138
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

40
00:04:19,608 --> 00:04:22,904
Uptime says zero minutes, so this really is a fresh boot.

41
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:25,988
The checker later confirms the files are still there.

42
00:04:31,508 --> 00:04:35,027
Your turn. Create a directory called lab3.

43
00:04:35,047 --> 00:04:39,915
Run L S on slash etc slash hostname and slash nonexistent,

44
00:04:39,935 --> 00:04:46,656
so that normal output goes to ok dot t x t, and the error goes to err dot t x t.

45
00:04:49,626 --> 00:04:54,833
Both files go in lab3. Then run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

46
00:05:00,853 --> 00:05:04,609
Next up: Use grep and regular expressions to analyze text.

47
00:05:05,079 --> 00:05:07,982
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
